  <div class="mt-8 space-y-8 text-body dark:text-muted">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

    <!-- Summary Box -->
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft p-6 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mb-3">
        Quick Executive Summary: E33G Digital Nomad Tax Rules
      </h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        <li>
          <strong>E33G Remote Worker Visa:</strong> Introduced by Indonesia's Directorate General of Immigration, allowing foreign remote workers earning at least $60,000 USD/year from offshore employers to live legally in Bali and across Indonesia for up to 1 year (renewable for up to 2 years).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tax Status Under 183 Days:</strong> Foreign remote workers staying 182 days or fewer in a rolling 12-month period remain non-residents (SPLN). Foreign-sourced salary remitted into offshore bank accounts is 100% exempt from Indonesian income tax.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The 183-Day Residency Cliff:</strong> Staying past 183 days triggers domestic tax resident status (SPDN) under Article 2 of the Income Tax Act (UU PPh) and Law No. 7/2021 (UU HPP), potentially subjecting worldwide earnings to progressive rates (5% to 35%).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The 4-Year Expat Territorial Shield:</strong> Foreigners possessing specific STEM or managerial skill sets can elect for territorial taxation for their first 4 years under Article 4(1a) of UU Cipta Kerja.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Hub CTA Card -->
