    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-10">
      Do E33G Remote Worker Visa Holders Pay Income Tax in Indonesia?
    </h2>
    <p>
      E33G Remote Worker Visa holders do not pay Indonesian income tax on remote earnings paid by offshore foreign employers, provided they do not conduct commercial business with Indonesian domestic clients or stay in Indonesia beyond 183 days without qualifying for the Omnibus Law expat territorial exemption.
    </p>
    <p>
      For years, digital nomads residing in Canggu, Ubud, and Uluwatu operated in a legal gray zone using Tourist Visas on Arrival (B1/VoA) or Pre-Investment Visas (C11/B211A). The Indonesian Directorate General of Immigration addressed this ambiguity by launching the <strong>E33G Remote Worker Visa</strong>. This dedicated visa explicitly allows foreign employees and freelancers to reside in Indonesia while working exclusively for companies outside Indonesia.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, immigration legality does not automatically overwrite fiscal law. Indonesia's tax code is governed separately by the <em>Direktorat Jenderal Pajak (DJP)</em> under the Ministry of Finance. Below is the operational comparison of visa rules versus tax obligations:
    </p>

    <!-- Comparison Table -->
    <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-canvas-soft dark:bg-surface-soft border-b border-hairline dark:border-overlay-lighter text-ink dark:text-on-primary font-bold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3 sm:p-4">Visa &amp; Tax Parameter</th>
            <th class="p-3 sm:p-4">Short Stay (&le; 183 Days)</th>
