            <td class="p-3 sm:p-4">5% – 35% Progressive (or 4-Year Expat Exemption)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 sm:p-4 font-semibold">NPWP Tax Registration</td>
            <td class="p-3 sm:p-4">Not Required</td>
            <td class="p-3 sm:p-4">Mandatory within 30 days of crossing 183 days</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 sm:p-4 font-semibold">Annual Tax Return (SPT Tahunan)</td>
            <td class="p-3 sm:p-4">No Filing Required</td>
            <td class="p-3 sm:p-4">Mandatory via DJP Online before March 31</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 sm:p-4 font-semibold">Indonesian Source Income Tax</td>
            <td class="p-3 sm:p-4">Flat 20% PPh 26 Withholding</td>
            <td class="p-3 sm:p-4">5% – 35% Progressive PPh 21 Rates</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-10">
      What Triggers Indonesian Tax Residency for Digital Nomads?
    </h2>
    <p>
      An expatriate becomes an Indonesian domestic tax resident (Subjek Pajak Dalam Negeri / SPDN) immediately upon staying in the country for more than 183 days within any consecutive 12-month period, or by signing a long-term contract or lease establishing legal intent to reside.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under Article 2, Paragraph 3 of the Income Tax Act (UU PPh), physical presence is measured on a rolling 365-day basis, not a calendar year. This catches many digital nomads off-guard:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li>
        <strong>The Rolling Period Trap:</strong> If you arrive in Bali on October 1, 2025, and stay continuously through June 1, 2026, you have logged 244 days within an 8-month window. You have legally triggered tax residency, even though your stay was split across two separate calendar tax years.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Intent to Reside Clause:</strong> If you enter on a working KITAS or sign a 2-year villa lease with your family, DJP tax officers can classify you as a tax resident from day one based on demonstrable intent.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-10">
      How Does the 4-Year Territorial Expat Tax Exemption Work?
    </h2>
    <p>
      Under Law No. 7/2021 on the Harmonization of Tax Regulations (UU HPP) amending the Job Creation Law, qualifying foreign professionals residing in Indonesia are taxed exclusively on Indonesian-sourced income for their first 4 tax years, completely shielding their overseas foreign investments and salaries from Indonesian progressive rates.
    </p>
    <p>
      This represents one of Indonesia's most significant tax reforms, specifically designed to attract foreign capital, technology founders, and highly skilled specialists. To qualify for this 4-year territorial tax protection:
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
      <li>
        <strong>Specialized Skill Requirement:</strong> The foreign national must possess specialized skills in science, technology, engineering, mathematics (STEM), or corporate leadership as regulated under Minister of Finance Regulation No. 18/PMK.03/2021.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Offshore Sourcing Test:</strong> The foreign income must originate entirely outside Indonesia and cannot be paid by an Indonesian permanent establishment (BUT) or related Indonesian subsidiary.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No Double Tax Treaty Abuse:</strong> The taxpayer cannot claim dual treaty relief on the same shielded income in their home jurisdiction.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft p-5 my-6 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
      <h4 class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mb-2 text-sm sm:text-base">
        Important Caveat on the 4-Year Exemption for E33G Nomads:
      </h4>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">
        The 4-year exemption requires an active application and verification of professional credentials through the DJP. If you are an unregistered freelancer living in Bali who crosses 183 days without formal expat qualification, the DJP default position is to treat you as a standard domestic tax resident subject to worldwide income reporting on Form 1770.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-10">
      What are the Indonesian Personal Income Tax Brackets for 2026?
    </h2>
    <p>
      Indonesian tax residents are taxed on net chargeable income at progressive rates ranging from 5% up to 35% across five statutory income tiers, after deducting the Non-Taxable Income allowance (Penghasilan Tidak Kena Pajak / PTKP).
    </p>
    <p>
      Chargeable income is calculated after applying standard deductions, including the basic personal PTKP of IDR 54,000,000 for single taxpayers, plus IDR 4,500,000 for married taxpayers and IDR 4,500,000 per child (up to 3 dependents):
    </p>

    <!-- Tax Slabs Table -->
    <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-canvas-soft dark:bg-surface-soft border-b border-hairline dark:border-overlay-lighter text-ink dark:text-on-primary font-bold">
          <tr>
